What happens when state changes?
when state changes the react will schedule a rerender a component
During rerender the component the react uses updated state value and 
update necessary part of ui 

Can we have multiple useStates?
We can have multiple state variables in one component
const [name, setName] = useState("");
const [age, setAge] = useState(0);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

For example, I can have separate states for form data, loading status, selected values, and error messages. Each state has its own setter function.

useEffect
=========
useEffect is a Reacthook, it is used to perform the side effects in a functional component

one common usecase is Api integration, when component loads i will call the backend api using the axios/fetch
The request goes to the API URL, then the Django REST Framework URL routes the request to the appropriate view. The view processes the request, interacts with the database if required, and sends the response back to React. I can then store the response in state using useState

